{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes two active mechanics. First, a pack was purchased accidentally through a double tap, implying that the purchase flow lacked sufficient confirmation or made unintended spending too easy. Second, after the refund, the game locked the account and required repayment of the refunded amount before access could be restored, making continued play contingent on payment. The review does not describe a recurring fee, currency conversion, or a technical fault causing the purchase or lock.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "accidentally brought a pack when I double tapped the screen",
      "rule_applied": "Accidental Purchases: making it easy to accidentally spend money with no confirmation or ability to undo or refund the action.",
      "rationale": "The wording indicates that a simple double tap caused an unintended purchase, showing an overly frictionless purchase flow."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "now my game has been locked and they are holding my account to ransom until I pay the equivalent of the refund I got",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall: the game reaches a point where further progress or continued play is contingent on making a payment.",
      "rationale": "The account is locked and access to the game is withheld until the reviewer pays money."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a competitive dice game in which players face one another to determine a winner. It also explicitly alleges that the outcome is fixed in advance: the game chooses which player will win at the start and then manipulates the dice rolls to produce that result. The predictable pattern of one player receiving mostly sixes and another mostly ones or twos is presented as evidence of concealed, non-random outcome determination, so this supports Illusion of Control. No monetary stake, reward-delivery system, or merely unlucky streak is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "which player has to win",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes players competing against one another for a winner-determined match outcome."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "Game randomly decides at the start of the play that which player has to win and dice rolls according to it.",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The span alleges that the game secretly determines the winner in advance and tunes the dice rolls to that hidden outcome."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game download system that requires an additional download after the reviewer believed the game file was already downloaded. This obscures or fragments the game's true download requirements, matching Fragmented Downloads. The later matchmaking problem is explicitly attributed to a Wi-Fi or technical problem, so it does not support a dark-pattern label under R11. The mention of matchmaking does not by itself describe a competition mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "Tech_FragmentedDownloads",
      "span": "I thought I already download the file but I still get an additional download",
      "rule_applied": "Small advertised App Store size vs. large mandatory in-game download / fragmented in-game downloads",
      "rationale": "The game requires another download after the reviewer believed the necessary file had already been downloaded, obscuring the true download requirements."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a merge game whose board becomes blocked by accumulating items, and whose new archiving feature still consumes limited inventory capacity, leaving deletion as the apparent way to make room. That is an explicit inventory-capacity problem with value being forced out of the game, so it supports Waste Aversion. The mention of an ad is external marketing and is excluded by R13. Energy scarcity is mentioned, but no regeneration timer, paid refill, or required waiting schedule is described, so it does not support Playing by Appointment or Pay to Progress. Unlocking new items is not described as repetitive enough to support Grinding.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "they finally started \"archiving\" but expect you to use your inventory space for them??? Hello?!? DELETE.",
      "rule_applied": "M_WasteAversion definition and boundary rule: capped inventory forces players to destroy items or otherwise forfeit held value; the span explicitly describes archive items consuming inventory space and deletion as the resulting remedy.",
      "rationale": "Archived items still occupy limited inventory capacity, creating a realized space shortfall that appears to force deletion of items."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes advertisements appearing automatically after each over and each fallen wicket, making the player encounter repeated in-game ads during normal play. This supports Advertisement because the ads are imposed at recurring gameplay points. The complaint that the opponent catches easily while the player's team drops catches expresses perceived unfairness, but it does not explicitly describe rigging, concealed outcome determination, or another coded mechanic. The references to wasting data and time are general evaluations rather than additional mechanics.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "Too much ads are shown after every over and every wicket fell.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Ads are automatically shown during normal gameplay after recurring cricket events, compelling the player to encounter them."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes repeated fights being required to unlock additional heroes, with nearly 100 fights producing little or no progress beyond the two starting heroes. This is a repetitive activity tied directly to advancement, so it supports Grinding. The failed \"share to unlock\" behavior is explicitly reported as not working, making it a bug or technical fault rather than a deliberate dark pattern. The review does not establish payment, social recruitment, messaging, or competition against other players.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "I have done close to 100 fights and still have the first two heros you get when you start the game",
      "rule_applied": "vs Slow Progress: assign Grinding where the span describes the activity required for progress as repetitive or requiring repeated attempts.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer has repeatedly completed fights to unlock heroes, showing a monotonous progress activity rather than merely stating that progress is slow."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a recurring content schedule: ordinary level progression stops for two weeks, then only about 50 new levels are released before the player reaches another endpoint and must wait two more weeks. The wait is an in-game time gate, supporting Playing by Appointment. The repeated release of limited batches means the endpoint continually recedes rather than allowing completion, supporting Infinite Treadmill. The review also names Champions League; I searched because its mechanics are not clear from the review alone. The official Toon Blast help page establishes that Champions League places users in a league with other users and ranks them by trophies, supporting Competition. ([peakgames.helpshift.com](https://peakgames.helpshift.com/hc/en/4-toon-blast/faq/25-what-is-the-champions-league/?f=what-is-the-team-tournament&l=en&p=android&s=events&utm_source=openai)) The phrases about a money grab and losing the bankroll express dissatisfaction and spending, but do not describe a specific monetization mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "have to wait another 2 weeks for levels",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players arbitrarily wait for something.",
      "rationale": "The game imposes a two-week real-world wait before the player can continue to new levels."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Get new levels on 3/2/26 only to go maybe 50 levels and no more levels.",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: content is supplied in recurring batches so the endpoint continually recedes and the game does not reach a stable completion point.",
      "rationale": "The player reaches the end of a small batch, after which further progress depends on another future release."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "get a champions league",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: the game makes players compete against other players; R10 search resolution established Champions League as a league where users compete by collecting trophies. ([peakgames.helpshift.com](https://peakgames.helpshift.com/hc/en/4-toon-blast/faq/25-what-is-the-champions-league/?f=what-is-the-team-tournament&l=en&p=android&s=events&utm_source=openai))",
      "rationale": "The named Champions League is a competitive league involving other users."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Toon Blast Champions League event competition",
  "search_result": "The official Toon Blast Help Center states that Champions League places users in a league with other users, where trophy totals determine rankings and rewards. ([peakgames.helpshift.com](https://peakgames.helpshift.com/hc/en/4-toon-blast/faq/25-what-is-the-champions-league/?f=what-is-the-team-tournament&l=en&p=android&s=events&utm_source=openai))"
}

{
  "analysis": "The external advertisements are described as more entertaining than the game, but under R13 that out-of-game marketing is excluded. Within the game, the reviewer describes a repetitive loop of clicking upgrades, conducting research, and moving to locations, with these activities occupying essentially all gameplay. The mention of daily quests only describes using them to keep track of numerous tasks; it does not establish a daily reward incentive or penalty.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "all the game ever had me do what click to upgrade this or research that or go there",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Grinding applies where the span identifies repetitive or monotonous activities required for game advancement.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes gameplay as an ongoing cycle of repetitive upgrade, research, and movement tasks."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes three relevant mechanics or possible mechanics. The game presents limited-time purchase deals on login, which uses artificial scarcity and urgency. It also states that paying is the best way to win, indicating that spending provides an in-game advantage and supports Pay to Progress. The referral mention describes receiving a free champion and silver, but it does not explicitly say the player is rewarded for inviting new players, so Forced Fellowship is not assigned. The login pop-ups are purchase solicitations, not forced ad viewing or a clearly described friction-reducing purchase interface, so Advertisement and Easy to Purchase are not assigned. Finally, calling the late game a grind does not identify a specific repetitive task required for progress, so Grinding is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_DeceptiveLuxury",
      "span": "\"limited time\" deals",
      "rule_applied": "Artificial Scarcity: limited-time offers with unnecessary urgency.",
      "rationale": "The game frames purchase offers as limited-time deals, creating urgency through manufactured scarcity."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "p2p is the best way to win this game.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that paying is the best route to winning, implying a gameplay advantage from spending."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a board-capacity constraint: limited space makes it necessary to purchase additional storage with diamonds, and each successive storage purchase costs more. This is a concrete inventory-capacity shortfall paired with a paid expansion, supporting Waste Aversion. The reviewer also says they spent a lot of energy obtaining needed items, but does not explicitly describe repetitive tasks, timers, or a monetary conversion for the diamonds. The potterwheel is named but its exact mechanic is not clear enough to support another label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "The limited space is bothersome too and you have to use diamonds to buy more storage with each purchase the price gets higher.",
      "rule_applied": "Capped inventory forces you to destroy items or upgrade inventory; Waste Aversion applies where a capacity shortfall leads to purchasing additional storage.",
      "rationale": "The limited board capacity creates insufficient storage, and the player must buy progressively more expensive storage upgrades to address it."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes advertisements presented within the game and a daily reward that can only be claimed after watching an ad. The ad requirement is an in-game compelled or incentivized advertisement, while the daily reward is a daily-return reward mechanic. The mention of paid popups alone does not establish a specific purchase-friction mechanism, so it is not coded as Easy to Purchase.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "ask u to watch an ad just to claim a daily reward",
      "rule_applied": "Forced to watch ads or given rewards for watching ads",
      "rationale": "The player must watch an advertisement to obtain an in-game reward."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "claim a daily reward",
      "rule_applied": "Daily log-in reward calendars",
      "rationale": "The span identifies a recurring daily reward available for claiming."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement that is forced after every cricket over, so advertising interrupts normal gameplay. The app closing after an accidental tap is attributed to bad app behavior or a technical issue, so that malfunction does not create a separate dark-pattern label and does not suppress the independent forced-ad mechanic. The request to stop the ads or fix the UI is hypothetical and is not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "After every over, an ad pops up.",
      "rule_applied": "Forced to watch ads",
      "rationale": "An advertisement is imposed during gameplay after every over, describing a forced in-game ad interruption."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes item-recharge and cooking timers that prevent immediate play, requiring the player to wait for the game’s schedule. It also describes watching advertisements in exchange for a limited recharge, which is an incentivized ad mechanic. Finally, the board has limited space, forcing the player to delete items they have already invested time in; this is realized wasted value from inventory constraints. The statement that the game is a money grab is general dissatisfaction and does not independently establish Pay to Progress.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the time it takes to recharge items and how long it takes to 'cook'",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players wait for something.",
      "rationale": "Item recharge and cooking timers impose real-time waits before the player can continue using those game functions."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "I have to watch adds for a measly recharge",
      "rule_applied": "Forced or incentivized advertisements: players are compelled to watch ads or given rewards for watching them.",
      "rationale": "The player watches an advertisement to receive an in-game recharge."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "I run out of space on the board and have to delete items I spent time on",
      "rule_applied": "Capped inventory forces players to destroy items or upgrade inventory; assign when the loss of held value is explicitly realized.",
      "rationale": "Limited board capacity forces the player to discard items representing time already invested."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progress being blocked unless the player makes real-money purchases. It also describes card packs as a collection the player is expected to complete, with completion said to be impossible without payment. The energy cap limits how far the player can advance through an order, but no explicit timer or regeneration schedule is stated, so Playing by Appointment is not assigned. The mention of gems does not clearly state that real money is converted into gems, so Intermediate Currency is not assigned. General frustration about feeling trapped into spending does not by itself establish Easy to Purchase or another label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "you can't do anything without using in app purchases. you will never be able to complete the card packs and other mini games without being forced to pay real money for it.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall: the game reaches a point where further progress is contingent on making a payment; Free alternative boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly says continued activity and completion of game content are impossible without real-money payment."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "you will never be able to complete the card packs",
      "rule_applied": "definition: the compulsion to acquire all the items, achievements, or hidden features in a game",
      "rationale": "Card packs are described as a collection that the player is trying to complete."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a login streak that rewards returning to the game daily and resets or is lost when the system records a missed login. Although the reviewer says the loss occurred \"for no reason\" and denies missing the previous day, the underlying mechanic is still a daily login streak with a penalty for a missed or falsely detected day. No progression gate or real-time wait is described, so Playing by Appointment does not apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I had a login streak of 53 that I just lost today for no reason. I did not miss yesterday's login.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; R11",
      "rationale": "A login streak is a daily-return incentive, and losing it is the penalty associated with a missed or incorrectly recorded login."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review explicitly describes a pay-to-win structure, where spending money provides an advantage. It also describes player-versus-player game modes in which stronger players take resources from weaker players. Because the harm is asymmetric and the stronger aggressor benefits by stealing from weaker players, this goes beyond ordinary competition and indicates design that encourages antisocial behavior. The statement that money is the only way to bridge the divide reinforces the payment-based advantage but does not add a separate label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Extremely pay2win",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly states that spending creates an in-game advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "a bunch of game modes where strong players steal from weaker players",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "These game modes make players compete against one another."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_EncouragesAntiSocialBehavior",
      "span": "strong players steal from weaker players",
      "rule_applied": "vs Permitted harm; assign where the span carries asymmetric harmful conduct and the gain it produces",
      "rationale": "The design rewards or enables stronger players to harm and take from weaker players, describing predatory conduct beyond ordinary competition."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game schedule that demands repeated logins every 2–3 hours or continuous presence, making the player conform to the game's timing rather than their own. The warning about missing treasure digs adds anticipatory pressure not to stop playing, so Fear of Missing Out also applies. The statement that competition is determined by who spends the most describes player competition and a monetized rivalry in which spending provides the competitive advantage, supporting Competition and Pay to Progress. Calling the game a gacha game identifies a chance-based reward delivery system, supporting Reward Mania; no explicit monetary stake in the gacha mechanic is described, so Gambling is not assigned. The claim that the game is falsely advertised and that screenshots were added to avoid chargebacks concerns external marketing and is out of scope under R13.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the pressure to login every 2-3 hours 24/7 is constant with Arms Race or keep it open all day long",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play / definition: patterns that force players to adhere to the game's schedule.",
      "rationale": "The game requires repeated logins at specific intervals or continuous availability, imposing its schedule on the player."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FearOfMissingOutFOMO",
      "span": "so you don't miss treasure digs",
      "rule_applied": "definition: players are pressured to continue playing by fear of missing out on rewards or events.",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes continued play to avoid missing a time-sensitive in-game opportunity."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "Competing=who spends the most.",
      "rule_applied": "definition: the game makes players compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes competition between players based on spending."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Competing=who spends the most.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win / Monetized Rivalries: spending real money to gain an advantage or achieve in-game status in competition.",
      "rationale": "The span implies that greater spending determines competitive advantage or placement."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "This is a gacha game",
      "rule_applied": "Gacha mechanics like pulls, rolls, banners.",
      "rationale": "Calling the game a gacha game identifies a chance-based reward delivery system that sustains engagement."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a multiplayer combat system in which other players can destroy the reviewer's base, so the game places players in direct competition. It also describes troops and defenses that require months of upgrading before being nerfed, meaning invested game assets lose value after acquisition through balancing updates; this matches Power Creep. The mentions of bugs, sales, support, differing reward amounts, and general frustration do not identify additional qualifying mechanics. The long play history and chore-like experience do not by themselves show Grinding or Easy to Get, Hard to Lose.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "I have a maxed out account and anyone can destroy me.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes other players attacking and destroying the player's account, showing direct player-versus-player competition."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "You will spend months upgrading troops and defenses just to be nerfed.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Power Creep requires acquired game assets to lose value through later balancing or nerfing.",
      "rationale": "Troops and defenses that took months to upgrade are subsequently nerfed, reducing the value of the player's prior investment."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a monetized chance-based summoning system: players may spend about $140 for only a 50% chance of obtaining a rate-up character, which supports Gambling. The slot-machine framing and the incentive to keep rolling for rewards that reduce grind support Reward Mania because engagement is sustained by repeated, unpredictable reward delivery. The statement that most characters are limited-time only describes artificial scarcity tied to obtaining them, supporting Deceptive Luxury. Finally, the game is described as making players grind in order to build sunk cost, implying accumulated time investment that makes leaving harder, supporting Easy to Get, Hard to Lose. The review does not identify a specific repetitive activity beyond the general word 'grind,' so Grinding is not assigned under its boundary rule; nor does it explicitly state that payment is required for progression outside the chance mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "hope you have godly luck, or fork over some cash (~$140 for 50% to get a 'rate up' character)",
      "rule_applied": "Loot Boxes / Gambling: spending real money to participate in a game of chance for a reward.",
      "rationale": "The player can spend real money for only a stated 50% chance of receiving a desired character."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "slot machine summoning system",
      "rule_applied": "Gacha mechanics like pulls, rolls, banners; Variable Rewards.",
      "rationale": "The summoning system is framed as a slot-machine-like, repeated chance-based reward mechanism that sustains engagement."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_DeceptiveLuxury",
      "span": "because the majority of them are limited time only",
      "rule_applied": "Artificial Scarcity: limited-time offers or unnecessary urgency affecting purchase decisions.",
      "rationale": "Characters are made available only temporarily, creating manufactured scarcity around obtaining them."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "Gameplay is mostly just grind to build sunk cost.",
      "rule_applied": "Invested (Endowed) Value: accumulated time investment makes it difficult to abandon the game.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes gameplay as building sunk cost, implying that accumulated investment raises the cost of quitting."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer mainly expresses dissatisfaction with the lack of real-time gameplay, which is an evaluation rather than a codable mechanic. The described in-game behavior is that, after returning, the player is presented with an unfamiliar flashing button that leads to a direct payment request. The flashing presentation directs attention toward the purchase option, supporting UI Misdirection. The review does not establish that payment is required for progress, that an advertisement must be watched, or that the purchase prompt was triggered by insufficient funds.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "a flashing button I didn't recognise. Clicked the button \"DO YOU WANT TO PAY US $8?!\"",
      "rule_applied": "Exciting UI Effect; the interface uses animation or flashing to direct attention toward a purchase option.",
      "rationale": "The unfamiliar flashing button focuses the player's attention on a payment prompt."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a solitaire draw mechanic that the player believes is deliberately manipulated: after reducing the board to one remaining card, drawing through the remaining deck still fails to provide the needed card, and purchasing additional cards leaves the same obstacle in place. The explicit claim that the game is \"rigged\" and the repeated outcome assertion support Illusion of Control because the game is said to conceal or manipulate how outcomes are determined. The review also mentions paying coins for more cards, but it does not clearly establish that real-world money is converted into those coins or that the payment is required for progression rather than an optional continuation, so no monetary label is assigned. No grinding, gambling, reward-delivery, or infinite-completion mechanic is clearly described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "This is incredibly rigged",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging: assign where the span asserts that the game conceals or manipulates the determination of outcomes.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly alleges that the game's card outcomes are rigged rather than determined fairly or randomly."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a leaderboard that ranks players against one another, so the game is explicitly competitive. Losing a level threatens both leaderboard position and accumulated winning-streak rewards, creating pressure to keep winning. The reviewer also says payment is the way to avoid dropping on the leaderboard, which describes spending for a competitive advantage or status. The threat of falling behind and losing rewards supports Fear of Missing Out. The statement that the game makes the player feel bad is an evaluation of the mechanic's emotional effect, not by itself a separate label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "force you to be competitive",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The game explicitly places players in competition through its leaderboard system."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "unless you pay up, you're going to go down on the leaderboard",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win / Monetized Rivalries indicator",
      "rationale": "Payment is presented as the way to preserve leaderboard standing and gain a competitive advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FearOfMissingOutFOMO",
      "span": "you're going to go down on the leaderboard and will lose all your winning streak rewards",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The game pressures the player by threatening that stopping or failing to win will cause them to fall behind and lose rewards."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes advertisements appearing inside the game despite the expectation that it would be ad free. More specifically, the player cannot simply dismiss the advertisement: leaving it requires opening the Play Store, creating an obstructed opt-out and forcing additional external interaction and data use. The pre-install hype about being ad free is marketing outside the in-game design scope, so only the active in-game advertisement and its dismissal behavior are coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
